<institution>Department of Cardiology, Ghent University Hospital</institution>, <addr-line>Corneel Heymanslaan 10, Ghent 9000</addr-line>, <country country="BE">Belgium</country></aff></contrib></contrib-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="editor"><name><surname>Monzo</surname><given-names>Luca</given-names></name><role>Handling Editor</role></contrib><contrib contrib-type="editor"><name><surname>Kashimura</surname><given-names>Takeshi</given-names></name><role>Editor</role></contrib><contrib contrib-type="editor"><name><surname>Ricci</surname><given-names>Piera</given-names></name><role>Editor</role></contrib></contrib-group><author-notes><corresp id="ytaf620-cor1">Corresponding author. Tel: +32 9 332 33 44, Email: <email>anais.carlier@ugent.be</email></corresp><fn id="FN01" fn-type="COI-statement"><p>
<italic toggle="yes">Conflict of interest</italic>. None declared.</p></fn></author-notes><pub-date pub-type="collection"><month>12</month><year>2025</year></pub-date><pub-date pub-type="epub" iso-8601-date="2025-12-01"><day>01</day><month>12</month><year>2025</year></pub-date><pub-date pub-type="pmc-release"><day>01</day><month>12</month><year>2025</year></pub-date><volume>9</volume><issue>12</issue><elocation-id>ytaf620</elocation-id><history><date date-type="received"><day>17</day><month>5</month><year>2025</year></date><date date-type="rev-recd"><day>19</day><month>8</month><year>2025</year></date><date date-type="accepted"><day>22</day><month>11</month><year>2025</year></date><date date-type="corrected-typeset"><day>09</day><month>12</month><year>2025</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>&#x000a9; The Author(s) 2025. Published by Oxford University Press on behalf of the European Society of Cardiology.</copyright-statement><copyright-year>2025</copyright-year><license><ali:license_ref xmlns:ali="http://www.niso.org/schemas/ali/1.0/" specific-use="textmining" content-type="ccbynclicense">https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/</ali:license_ref><license-p>This is an Open Access article distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial License (<ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/">https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/</ext-link>), which permits non-commercial re-use, distribution, and reproduction in any medium, provided the original work is properly cited. For commercial re-use, please contact reprints@oup.com for reprints and translation rights for reprints. All other permissions can be obtained through our RightsLink service via the Permissions link on the article page on our site&#x02014;for further information please contact journals.permissions@oup.com.</license-p></license></permissions><self-uri xlink:href="ytaf620.pdf"/><abstract><title>Abstract</title><sec id="s1"><title>Background</title><p>Pulmonary arterial hypertension (PAH) is a rare but potentially severe manifestation in patients with connective tissue diseases (CTDs) like systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE). While immunosuppressive therapy is not indicated in most PAH subtypes, it plays a pivotal role in SLE-associated PAH due to its immune-mediated pathophysiology.</p></sec><sec id="s2"><title>Case summary</title><p>This case report describes a 43-year-old woman with SLE who experienced two severe PAH flares, each following discontinuation of maintenance immunosuppression. The first episode was complicated by circulatory shock following &#x003b2;-blocker administration and treated with a combination of corticosteroids and pulmonary vasodilators. The second episode responded well to immunosuppressive therapy alone, without the need to escalate pulmonary vasodilator treatment.</p></sec><sec id="s3"><title>Discussion</title><p>Both SLE-PAH flares followed discontinuation of maintenance immunosuppressive therapy, underscoring that continuous immunosuppression might reduce flare risk. The clinical course during flares aligns with evidence that SLE-associated PAH benefits from immunosuppression, in contrast to other PAH forms. Finally, the case highlights that negative chronotropic-inotropic agents (e.g. &#x003b2;-blockers) can induce life-threatening haemodynamic shock in patients with PAH.</p></sec></abstract><kwd-group><kwd>Case report</kwd><kwd>Pulmonary arterial hypertension</kwd><kwd>Systemic lupus erythematosus</kwd><kwd>Connective tissue disease</kwd><kwd>Immunosuppressive therapy</kwd><kwd>&#x00392;-blockers</kwd></kwd-group><counts><page-count count="5"/></counts></article-meta></front><body><boxed-text id="ytaf620-box1" position="anchor"><label>Learning points</label><list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Continuous immunosuppressive therapy in SLE might reduce flare risk and should be maintained whenever possible.</p></list-item><list-item><p>SLE-associated PAH, unlike other PAH forms, responds well to immunosuppression.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Negative chronotropic-inotropic agents (e.g. &#x003b2;-blockers) can induce life-threatening haemodynamic shock in patients with PAH.</p></list-item></list></boxed-text><sec sec-type="intro" id="ytaf620-s1"><title>Introduction</title><p>Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) is a chronic autoimmune connective tissue disease (CTD) with multisystem involvement, affecting the skin, joints, kidneys and cardiovascular system.<sup><xref rid="ytaf620-B1" ref-type="bibr">1&#x02013;4</xref></sup></p><p>Pulmonary hypertension (PH) is a potentially life-threatening pulmonary vascular complication of SLE, defined by a mean pulmonary artery pressure (mPAP) &#x0003e; 20&#x02005;mmHg at right heart catheterization (RHC). Pulmonary arterial hypertension (PAH), a pre-capillary form of PH (group 1, <italic toggle="yes"><xref rid="ytaf620-T1" ref-type="table">Table 1</xref></italic>) is characterized by progressive pulmonary vascular remodeling and increased resistance, leading to RV failure and death if untreated. RHC remains the diagnostic gold standard (<italic toggle="yes"><xref rid="ytaf620-T2" ref-type="table">Table 2</xref></italic>).<sup><xref rid="ytaf620-B1" ref-type="bibr">1</xref>,<xref rid="ytaf620-B4" ref-type="bibr">4&#x02013;12</xref></sup></p><table-wrap position="float" id="ytaf620-T1"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Clinical classification of pulmonary hypertension, adopted from humbert, kovacs<sup><xref rid="ytaf620-B5" ref-type="bibr">5</xref></sup></p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><colgroup span="1"><col align="left" span="1"/></colgroup><tbody><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">GROUP 1&#x02014;Pulmonary arterial hypertension</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.1 Idiopathic<break/>1.1.1. Non-responders at vasoreactivity testing<break/>1.1.2. Acute responders at vasoreactivity testing</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.2 Heritable</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.3 Associated with drugs and toxins</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.4 Associated with:<break/>1.4.1 Connective tissue disease<break/>1.4.2 HIV infection<break/>1.4.3 Portal hypertension<break/>1.4.4 Congenital heart disease<break/>1.4.5 Schistosomiasis</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.5 PAH with features of venous/capillary (PVOD/PCH) involvement</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.6 Persistent PH of the newborn</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">GROUP 2&#x02014;PH associated with left heart disease</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">2.1 Heart failure:<break/>2.1.1 With preserved ejection fraction<break/>2.1.2 With reduced or mildly reduced ejection fraction</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">2.2 Valvular heart disease</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">2.3 Congenital/acquired cardiovascular conditions leading to post-capillary PH</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">GROUP 3&#x02014;PH associated with lung diseases and/or hypoxia</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">3.1 Obstructive lung disease or emphysema</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">3.2 Restrictive lung disease</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">3.3 Lung disease with mixed restrictive/obstructive pattern</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">3.4 Hypoventilation syndromes</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">3.5 Hypoxia without lung disease (e.g. high altitude)</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">3.6 Developmental lung disorders</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">GROUP 4&#x02014;PH associated with pulmonary artery obstructions</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">4.1 Chronic thrombo-embolic PH</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">4.2 Other pulmonary artery obstructions</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">GROUP 5&#x02014;PH with unclear and/or multifactorial mechanisms</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">5.1 Haematological disorders</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">5.2 Systemic disorders</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">5.3 Metabolic disorders</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">5.4 Chronic renal failure with or without haemodialysis</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">5.5 Pulmonary tumour thrombotic microangiopathy</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">5.6 Fibrosing mediastinitis</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><fn id="tblfn1"><p>HIV, human immunodeficiency virus; PAH, pulmonary arterial hypertension; PCH, pulmonary capillary haemangiomatosis; PH, pulmonary hypertension; PVOD, pulmonary veno-occlusive disease.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><table-wrap position="float" id="ytaf620-T2"><label>Table 2</label><caption><p>Haemodynamic definition of pre-capillary pulmonary hypertension, adopted from humbert, kovacs<sup><xref rid="ytaf620-B5" ref-type="bibr">5</xref></sup></p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><colgroup span="1"><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="left" span="1"/></colgroup><thead><tr><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Haemodynamic characteristic</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">mPAP</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x0003e;20&#x02005;mmHg</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">PAWP</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02264;15&#x02005;mmHg</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">PVR</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x0003e;2 WU</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><fn id="tblfn2"><p>mPAP, mean pulmonary artery pressure; PAWP, pulmonary arterial wedge pressure; PVR, pulmonary vascular resistance; WU, Wood units.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><p>After idiopathic PAH (IPAH), CTD-associated PAH (CTD-PAH) is the second most prevalent type in Western countries.<sup><xref rid="ytaf620-B5" ref-type="bibr">5</xref>,<xref rid="ytaf620-B8" ref-type="bibr">8</xref>,<xref rid="ytaf620-B9" ref-type="bibr">9</xref>,<xref rid="ytaf620-B11" ref-type="bibr">11</xref></sup> Systemic sclerosis (SSc) accounts for most CTD-PAH cases, whereas in Asia, SLE is more frequent (15%&#x02013;20% of CTD-PAH). Compared with SSc-PAH, which follows an insidious fibrotic course, SLE-PAH is more immune-mediated and potentially reversible with timely immunosuppression.<sup><xref rid="ytaf620-B6" ref-type="bibr">6</xref>,<xref rid="ytaf620-B11" ref-type="bibr">11</xref></sup> While vasodilator therapy forms the cornerstone in idiopathic and SSc-PAH, immunosuppressive therapy plays a central role in SLE-PAH due to its autoimmune pathophysiology.<sup><xref rid="ytaf620-B4" ref-type="bibr">4&#x02013;6</xref>,<xref rid="ytaf620-B8" ref-type="bibr">8</xref>,<xref rid="ytaf620-B9" ref-type="bibr">9</xref>,<xref rid="ytaf620-B11" ref-type="bibr">11</xref>,<xref rid="ytaf620-B12" ref-type="bibr">12</xref></sup></p></sec><sec id="ytaf620-s2"><title>Summary figure</title><fig position="float" id="ytaf620-F1" fig-type="figure"><graphic xlink:href="ytaf620il2" position="float"/></fig></sec><sec id="ytaf620-s3"><title>Case presentation</title><p>A 43-year-old woman with long-standing SLE presents a complex disease course marked by two severe SLE-PAH flares.</p><sec id="ytaf620-s3.1"><title>Age 16 (1998)</title><p>She was diagnosed with SLE and Hashimoto&#x02019;s thyroiditis and started hydroxychloroquine 200&#x02005;mg once daily, methotrexate 7.5&#x02005;mg once weekly, and levothyroxine 125&#x02005;&#x000b5;g once daily. For several years, she remained stable, with intermittent Raynaud&#x02019;s phenomenon and mild arthralgias/myalgias while adherent to therapy.</p></sec><sec id="ytaf620-s3.2"><title>Age 25 (2006)&#x02014;first flare</title><p>After discontinuing immunosuppression for 1&#x02013;2 months because of a pregnancy wish, she was admitted with fever, diffuse arthralgias, and worsening Raynaud&#x02019;s phenomenon. Lung and abdominal examinations were unremarkable. Cardiac auscultation revealed a rapid, regular rhythm. Vital signs: Blood pressure (BP) 115/80&#x02005;mmHg, heart rate (HR) 130 bpm, temperature (T) 38.6&#x000b0;C, SpO&#x02082; 96%. ECG: sinus tachycardia without further abnormalities. Laboratory tests showed an active SLE flare: elevated anti-dsDNA (245&#x02005;IU/mL), hypocomplementemia (C3 0.45&#x02005;g/L; C4 0.06&#x02005;g/L), anaemia (Hb 10.1&#x02005;g/dL), and an elevated ESR (72&#x02005;mm/h).</p><p>Due to sinus tachycardia (130 bpm) with disturbing palpitations, a single dose of propranolol 20&#x02005;mg was given orally. Within three hours, she developed a fast progressive haemodynamic instability [systolic blood pressure (SBP) &#x0003c; 100&#x02005;mmHg, cool clammy skin and restlessness]. Despite fluid and oxygen (2&#x02005;L/min) administration, hypotension and general condition worsened (SBP &#x0003c;90&#x02005;mmHg, progressive peripheral cyanosis and declining consciousness) and eventually leading to a collapse, necessitating intensive reanimation. She was intubated and transferred to the intensive care unit (ICU), where veno-arterial extracorporeal membrane oxygenation (VA-ECMO) and renal replacement therapy were initiated. Transthoracic echocardiogram (TTE) revealed right ventricular (RV) dilation with an estimated systolic pulmonary artery pressure (sPAP) of 85&#x02005;mmHg. Pulmonary embolism was excluded on CT angiography. RHC confirmed pre-capillary PH [mPAP 59&#x02005;mmHg, pulmonary arterial wedge pressure (PAWP) 12&#x02005;mmHg, cardiac output (CO) 5&#x02005;L/min, pulmonary vascular resistance (PVR) 9.4 Wood units (WU)] without vasoreactivity. No left-sided heart disease, interstitial lung disease, or chronic thromboembolic PH was identified, confirming SLE-associated PAH.</p><p>Treatment consisted of high-dose methylprednisolone (500&#x0202f;mg IV for 3 days, then 64&#x02005;mg tapering oral regimen), vasopressor and inotropic support (adrenaline 275&#x02005;ng/kg/min and milrinone 0.3&#x02005;&#x003bc;g/kg/min) and pulmonary vasodilators (inhaled nitric oxide and oral sildenafil 25&#x0202f;mg three times daily). RV function and pulmonary pressures improved steadily. At discharge, sPAP was 67&#x02005;mmHg and laboratory findings normalized. Methotrexate was discontinued because of teratogenic risk; hydroxychloroquine was increased to 200&#x02005;mg twice daily. Escalation to dual therapy with bosentan was proposed but declined for pregnancy-related concerns.</p><p>During the ICU stay, ischemia from profound hypotension and ECMO cannulation led to necrosis of the left lower leg requiring amputation. She recovered functionally and remained in World Health Organization functional class (WHO-FC) II for several years under sildenafil monotherapy.</p></sec><sec id="ytaf620-s3.3"><title>Age 32 (2013)&#x02014;second flare</title><p>During extended travel abroad, she ran out of hydroxychloroquine for 1 month. She presented with fever and progressive dyspnoea. On admission: BP 118/76&#x02005;mmHg, HR 98 bpm, T 38.1&#x000b0;C, SpO&#x02082; 96%. Systemic examination (lungs, abdomen, and heart) was unremarkable. Laboratory testing indicated an active SLE flare: anti-dsDNA 198&#x02005;IU/mL, C3 0.50&#x02005;g/L, C4 0.07&#x02005;g/L, ESR 60&#x02005;mm/h. Echocardiography showed sPAP 105&#x02005;mmHg with RV dilation. As RHC was refused, corticosteroid monotherapy (methylprednisolone 32&#x02005;mg daily, tapering) was initiated, resulting after one week in a fall of sPAP to 73&#x02005;mmHg and clinical stabilization. Sildenafil 20&#x02005;mg TID was continued.</p></sec><sec id="ytaf620-s3.4"><title>Age 36 (2017)</title><p>A progressive decline in 6-minute walking distance (6MWD) was observed (488&#x0202f;m in 2014; 450&#x0202f;m in 2015; 434&#x0202f;m in 2016; 414&#x0202f;m in 2017) with increasing sPAP 93&#x02005;mmHg. RHC confirmed severe pre-capillary PAH (mPAP 66&#x0202f;mmHg, PAWP 13&#x02005;mmHg, CO 4.6&#x02005;L/min, PVR 11.5 WU). Macitentan 10&#x02005;mg once daily was added and sildenafil titrated to 40&#x02005;mg TID, resulting in improved 6MWD &#x0003e; 450&#x02005;m and reduced sPAP &#x0003c; 75&#x02005;mmHg. She remained WHO-FC II in subsequent years.</p></sec><sec id="ytaf620-s3.5"><title>Age 41 (2023)</title><p>A transient drop in 6MWD (410&#x02005;m) led to the proposal of prostacyclin-based triple therapy following repeat RHC. However, the patient declined given the fact she felt good and the fear for adverse drug effects. While we accept her decision to not escalate treatment within a shared decision-making framework, we consider her somehow undertreated and would ideally favor prostacyclin-based triple therapy. She is followed closely by a multidisciplinary team to enable timely intervention if needed.</p></sec><sec id="ytaf620-s3.6"><title>Age 43 (2025)</title><p>She currently remains WHO-FC II with NT-proBNP 559&#x02005;pg/mL and 6MWD 440&#x02005;m, classifying as intermediate-low risk (<italic toggle="yes"><xref rid="ytaf620-T3" ref-type="table">Table 3</xref></italic>). Current therapy includes hydroxychloroquine 200&#x02005;mg BID, sildenafil 40&#x02005;mg TID, macitentan 10&#x02005;mg daily, and levothyroxine 125&#x02005;&#x000b5;g daily. Regular follow-up every 4 months includes TTE, 6MWT, and laboratory evaluation.</p><table-wrap position="float" id="ytaf620-T3"><label>Table 3</label><caption><p>Simplified four-Strata-risk-assessment tool for 1-year mortality in PAH, adopted from humbert, kovacs.<sup><xref rid="ytaf620-B5" ref-type="bibr">5</xref></sup></p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><colgroup span="1"><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/></colgroup><thead><tr><th align="left" colspan="5" rowspan="1">a Variables used to calculate the simplified four-strata-risk-assessment tool, adopted from Humbert, Kovacs<sup><xref rid="ytaf620-B5" ref-type="bibr">5</xref></sup></th></tr><tr><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Determinants of prognosis</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Low risk</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Intermediate-low risk</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Intermediate-high risk</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">High risk</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Points assigned</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">1</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">2</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">3</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">4</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">WHO-FC</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">I or II</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02014;</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">III</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">IV</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">6MWD, m</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x0003e;440</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">320&#x02013;440</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">165&#x02013;319</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x0003c;165</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">BNP or</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x0003c;50</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">50&#x02013;199</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">200&#x02013;800</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x0003e;800</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">NT-proBNP, ng/L</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x0003c;300</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">300&#x02013;649</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">650&#x02013;1100</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x0003e;1100</td></tr><tr><td colspan="5" rowspan="1">6MWD, 6-minute walking distance; BNP, brain natriuretic peptide; NT-proBNP, <italic toggle="yes">n</italic>-terminal pro-brain natriuretic peptide; WHO-FC, World Health Organization functional class.</td></tr><tr><td colspan="5" rowspan="1">Risk is calculated by dividing the sum of all grades by the number of variables and rounding to the next integer.</td></tr></tbody></table><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><colgroup span="1"><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/></colgroup><thead><tr><th align="left" colspan="4" rowspan="1">b Four-Strata-Risk-Assessment Tool for the case patient as on April 2025.</th></tr><tr><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Determinants of prognosis</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Value of the patient</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Risk category</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Points</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">WHO-FC</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">II</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Low risk</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">1</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">6MWD, m</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">440</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Intermediate-low risk</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">2</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">NT-proBNP, ng/L</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">559</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Intermediate-low risk</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">2</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><fn id="tblfn3"><p>6MWD, 6-minute walking distance; NT-proBNP, <italic toggle="yes">n</italic>-terminal pro-brain natriuretic peptide; WHO-FC, World Health Organization functional class.</p></fn><fn id="tblfn4"><p>Risk is calculated by dividing the sum of all points (1 + 2 + 2 = 5) by the number of variables<sup><xref rid="ytaf620-B3" ref-type="bibr">3</xref></sup> and rounding to the next integer (&#x02248;2 = intermediate-low risk category).</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap></sec></sec><sec sec-type="discussion" id="ytaf620-s4"><title>Discussion</title><sec id="ytaf620-s4.1"><title>First flare</title><p>The first event occurred shortly after discontinuing immunosuppression because of pregnancy wish and concerns regarding teratogenicity. Timely communication about pregnancy planning and safe medication adjustment might have prevented this crisis. The episode exemplifies the 2023 European Alliance of Associations for Rheumatology (EULAR) recommendations advocating proactive reproductive counseling and multidisciplinary management in SLE.<sup><xref rid="ytaf620-B3" ref-type="bibr">3</xref></sup></p><p>Propranolol was administered to manage sinus tachycardia, however followed by acute cardiovascular collapse a couple of hours later. It was only afterwards that the diagnosis of severe PAH was made, likely pre-existing. Because patients with PAH depend on increased HR and contractility to offset elevated PVR and maintain cardiac output, &#x003b2;-blockade, by reducing chronotropy and inotropy, can precipitate a profound systemic hypotension.<sup><xref rid="ytaf620-B13" ref-type="bibr">13</xref></sup></p><p>Following this episode, RHC confirmed severe pre-capillary PAH. According to the 2004 European Society of Cardiology (ESC) and European Respiratory Society (ERS) guidelines, monotherapy was recommended for WHO-FC III&#x02013;IV patients.<sup><xref rid="ytaf620-B14" ref-type="bibr">14</xref></sup> At that time, the decision was made to give inhaled NO and oral sildenafil, and after weaning from ECMO, monotherapy with sildenafil was established. An escalation to dual therapy with bosentan was considered, but the patient declined due to concerns about its potential teratogenic effects. Nowadays, for patients presenting with high-risk PAH, guidelines recommend initial triple combination therapy.<sup><xref rid="ytaf620-B5" ref-type="bibr">5</xref></sup></p></sec><sec id="ytaf620-s4.2"><title>Second flare</title><p>Seven years later, interruption of hydroxychloroquine during travel again preceded a SLE flare with probable PAH progression (definitive PAH not established because RHC was declined). This interruption might have been preventable with pre-travel planning and an adequate prescription supply.</p><p>The 2009 ESC/ERS guidelines consider, besides PAH specific therapy, immunosuppressive therapy combining glucocorticosteroids and cyclophosphamide in patients with PAH associated with SLE or mixed CTD.<sup><xref rid="ytaf620-B15" ref-type="bibr">15</xref></sup> Since the patient refused a repeat RHC, the multidisciplinary decision was made to associate corticosteroids only. This led to stabilization of the patient&#x02019;s condition followed by tapering of corticosteroid therapy.</p></sec><sec id="ytaf620-s4.3"><title>Learning points</title><p>
<bold>1. Continuous immunosuppressive therapy in SLE</bold>
</p><p>Both SLE flares with severe PAH followed discontinuation of maintenance immunosuppressive therapy. While continuous maintenance therapy does not guarantee the absence of flares, adherence seems crucial for disease control. Therefore, a first learning point from this case is that sustained background control with continuous immunosuppression might reduce flare risk and should be maintained whenever possible.</p><p>
<bold>2. Immunosuppressants in SLE-PAH flares</bold>
</p><p>During the first flare, treatment with corticosteroids and pulmonary vasodilators were effective in controlling PAH. The second SLE flare with probable PAH progression responded well to immunosuppressive therapy alone, without the need to escalate pulmonary vasodilator treatment. This clinical course, in line with the literature, underscores a second learning point: SLE-associated PAH, unlike other PAH forms, responds well to immunosuppression.<sup><xref rid="ytaf620-B4" ref-type="bibr">4&#x02013;6</xref>,<xref rid="ytaf620-B8" ref-type="bibr">8</xref>,<xref rid="ytaf620-B9" ref-type="bibr">9</xref>,<xref rid="ytaf620-B11" ref-type="bibr">11</xref>,<xref rid="ytaf620-B12" ref-type="bibr">12</xref></sup> Alongside corticosteroids, guidelines endorse cyclophosphamide in SLE- or mixed-CTD-associated PAH.<sup><xref rid="ytaf620-B5" ref-type="bibr">5</xref>,<xref rid="ytaf620-B15" ref-type="bibr">15</xref></sup></p><p>
<bold>3. Negative inotropic-chronotropic agents in PAH</bold>
</p><p>During the first flare, PAH was unknowingly present, and the administration of a &#x003b2;-blocker caused a deep systemic hypotension, leading to cardiogenic shock requiring intensive reanimation and ICU admission. Profound hypotension and ECMO cannulation caused left lower leg ischemia with necrosis, leading to amputation.</p><p>These events have had lasting physical and psychological effects, contributing to the patient&#x02019;s reluctance to undergo invasive procedures such as RHC or to escalate therapy in subsequent years. Thus, a third and key learning point from this case: negative chronotropic-inotropic agents (e.g. &#x003b2;-blockers) can induce life-threatening haemodynamic shock in patients with PAH and is therefore contraindicated unless required by indicated comorbidities.<sup><xref rid="ytaf620-B5" ref-type="bibr">5</xref>,<xref rid="ytaf620-B13" ref-type="bibr">13</xref></sup></p></sec></sec><sec id="ytaf620-s5"><title>Lead author biography</title><p>
<inline-graphic xlink:href="ytaf620il1.jpg"/>
</p><p>Ana&#x000ef;s Carlier is a medical student at Ghent University with a broad interest in systemic diseases and their multidisciplinary approach.</p></sec></body><back><ack id="ack1"><title>Acknowledgements</title><p>We value the courage and the resilience of this patient. We are grateful to her for letting us share her story.</p><p>
<bold>Consent:</bold> The authors confirm that written consent for submission and publication of this case report including image(s) and associated text has been obtained from the patient in line with COPE guidance.</p></ack><sec><title>Funding</title><p>No funding was received for this work.</p></sec><sec sec-type="data-availability" id="ytaf620-s7"><title>Data availability</title><p>Data available on request.</p></sec><ref-list id="ref1"><title>References</title><ref id="ytaf620-B1"><label>1</label><mixed-citation publication-type="journal">
